 Full Port Scan
```
nmap -sC -sV -p- 10.129.178.147
```
The -p- flag scans all 65535 ports instead of just the default top 1000. This is critical because port 49663 — a second IIS web server — would have been completely missed with a default scan. Always do full port scans.
Key findings:
- Port 80, 49663 — Microsoft IIS 10.0 (Windows web server)
- Port 445 — SMB (Windows Server 2016)
- Port 3389 — RDP

 SMB Enumeration (Anonymous/Null Session)
bash
```
smbclient -L //10.129.178.147 -N
```
The -N flag attempts a null session — connecting with no credentials at all. This works when a server is misconfigured to allow anonymous access. The share nt4wrksv stood out immediately because it had no comment and was non-standard. Default shares like ADMIN,C, C ,C, IPC$ are expected — anything else is worth investigating.
